## Solution

**Setup.** Place the center of the rectangular hyperbola at the origin, and choose the coordinate axes along the (perpendicular) asymptotes. The hyperbola is then

$$xy = c^{2}\qquad (c>0).$$

A point on it is parametrized as $(ct,\, c/t)$, $t\neq 0$.

**The tangent.** Differentiating $xy=c^{2}$ gives $x\,dy+y\,dx=0$, hence at $(ct,c/t)$ the slope is $dy/dx = -y/x = -1/t^{2}$. The tangent line is

$$y-\frac{c}{t}=-\frac{1}{t^{2}}(x-ct)\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; x+t^{2}y=2ct,$$

i.e. $Ax+By+C=0$ with $A=1,\;B=t^{2},\;C=-2ct$.

**Foot of the perpendicular from the origin.** For a line $Ax+By+C=0$, the foot of the perpendicular from $(x_{0},y_{0})$ is
$\bigl(x_{0}-A\,\tfrac{Ax_{0}+By_{0}+C}{A^{2}+B^{2}},\; y_{0}-B\,\tfrac{Ax_{0}+By_{0}+C}{A^{2}+B^{2}}\bigr)$.
With $(x_{0},y_{0})=(0,0)$ and $Ax_{0}+By_{0}+C=C=-2ct$:

$$\boxed{\;x=\frac{2ct}{1+t^{4}},\qquad y=\frac{2ct^{3}}{1+t^{4}}\;}$$

This is the locus, parametrized by $t$.

**Conversion to polar coordinates.** Let $x=r\cos\theta,\;y=r\sin\theta$.

$$\frac{y}{x}=t^{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t^{2}=\tan\theta.$$

Also

$$r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}=\frac{4c^{2}t^{2}+4c^{2}t^{6}}{(1+t^{4})^{2}}=\frac{4c^{2}t^{2}(1+t^{4})}{(1+t^{4})^{2}}=\frac{4c^{2}t^{2}}{1+t^{4}}.$$

Substituting $t^{2}=\tan\theta$ and $t^{4}=\tan^{2}\theta$:

$$r^{2}=\frac{4c^{2}\tan\theta}{1+\tan^{2}\theta}=\frac{4c^{2}\tan\theta}{\sec^{2}\theta}=4c^{2}\sin\theta\cos\theta=2c^{2}\sin 2\theta.$$

Hence the polar equation of the locus is

$$\boxed{\;r^{2}=2c^{2}\sin 2\theta\;}$$

**Identification and sketch.** The equation $r^{2}=a^{2}\cos 2\varphi$ is the lemniscate of Bernoulli. Setting $\varphi=\theta-\pi/4$ and $a^{2}=2c^{2}$ gives $r^{2}=2c^{2}\cos(2\theta-\pi/2)=2c^{2}\sin 2\theta$. Thus the locus is a **lemniscate of Bernoulli**, rotated so that its axis lies along the line $y=x$ (the transverse axis of the hyperbola $xy=c^{2}$).

Since $r^{2}\ge 0$ requires $\sin 2\theta\ge 0$, we have $\theta\in[0,\pi/2]\cup[\pi,3\pi/2]$: two loops, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, meeting at the origin (which corresponds to $t\to 0$ and $t\to\infty$, i.e. the two asymptote directions). The maximum $r^{2}=2c^{2}$ is attained at $\theta=\pi/4$ and $\theta=5\pi/4$, i.e. at the points $(\pm c,\pm c)$ — precisely the vertices of the hyperbola, where the tangent is perpendicular to the radius vector.

Sketch: a figure-eight centered at the origin, symmetric about the line $y=x$ and about the origin, with each loop bulging out to distance $c\sqrt{2}$ from the origin along $y=x$ (first quadrant) and along $y=x$ (third quadrant), and pinching to the origin along the coordinate axes (the asymptotes of the hyperbola).

**Verification at a sample point.** At $t=1$ the hyperbola point is $(c,c)$, the tangent is $x+y=2c$, and the foot from the origin is $(c,c)$. The polar formula gives $r^{2}=2c^{2}\sin(\pi/2)=2c^{2}$, $\theta=\pi/4$, i.e. the point $(c,c)$. ✓

